# Anna's Appreciation in a Radio Interview

*(Appreciative remarks delivered by Anna in a Washington radio interview on 28 April 1968.)*

The people of America maintain a spirit of friendship toward the people of our country.

I could spend only a short time at the art gallery in Washington. Rare objects from around the world have been collected there, and thousands of people come from every region to see them.

When I visited the Abraham Lincoln Memorial in Washington, the many thoughts I had acquired from reading about him ripened and rose within my heart.

The killing by a fanatic of a great leader who laboured for democracy and disadvantaged people reminded me of a similar tragic event in our own country. Though Lincoln died, democracy remains evergreen among the American people through him.

We know that Abraham Lincoln was killed at Ford's Theatre, but what I saw there was a sight I had never encountered before. The theatre has been restored, and a beautiful Lincoln memorial has been established below it.

When the theatre staff explained where Lincoln watched the play and sat, how the assassin came and escaped, and which route he took, tears filled our eyes and our hearts melted. Sorrow overcame us as we reflected on the end met by such a leader.

When I visited, a Shakespeare play was being performed. Americans came not merely to see a play but with the sacred feeling that they were visiting the memorial of an incomparable leader.

During my stay in Washington, I met and conversed with people from many walks of American life. I met members of the Indian Embassy, spoke with them warmly, and exchanged valuable ideas.

My visits to New York, Yale, and Washington have left me inspired.

I met many Indians, including people from Tamil Nadu. I was happy to learn that they possess devotion to country and language and are eager to apply in our country the expertise they are acquiring in America.

*(Appreciative remarks delivered by Anna in a Washington radio interview on 28 April 1968.)*
